    <section class="sql-sheet-section">
        <div class="sql-section-heading">
            <span class="sql-section-number">1</span>
            <div>
                <h2>Normalization and Table Design</h2>
                <p>Read the structure before you write the queries.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <ol class="sql-task-list">
            <li class="sql-task">
                <div class="sql-task-top">
                    <span class="sql-task-tag">A3.2.5</span>
                    <span class="sql-task-focus">1NF to 3NF</span>
                </div>
                <p>Explain why <code>ORDER_ITEM</code> exists instead of storing several product names in one order row.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="sql-task">
                <div class="sql-task-top">
                    <span class="sql-task-tag">A3.2.5</span>
                    <span class="sql-task-focus">Dependencies</span>
                </div>
                <p>If <code>ProductName</code> and <code>Price</code> were copied into every order line, what update anomaly or duplication problem would appear?</p>
            </li>
            <li class="sql-task">
                <div class="sql-task-top">
                    <span class="sql-task-tag">A3.2.4</span>
                    <span class="sql-task-focus">Composite key</span>
                </div>
                <p>State the composite key in <code>ORDER_ITEM</code> and explain how it helps prevent duplicate line items for the same order.</p>
            </li>
            <li class="sql-task">
                <div class="sql-task-top">
                    <span class="sql-task-tag">A3.2.6</span>
                    <span class="sql-task-focus">3NF</span>
                </div>
                <p>Explain why splitting this system into <code>CUSTOMER</code>, <code>ORDER_HEADER</code>, <code>PRODUCT</code>, and <code>ORDER_ITEM</code> is closer to a 3NF design.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="sql-sheet-section">
